---
description: Do not add wrappers with no responsibility or reason to change
globs: **/*.ts,**/*.tsx
alwaysApply: true
---

# No zero-responsibility functions

Do not extract a function that only aliases a callee or adds a type
check. A function with nothing of its own to do has no reason to change
and makes the call site harder to read.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — typed alias of Object.fromEntries; 0 responsibility
const recordFromPairs = (pairs: Iterable<readonly [string, V]>): Record<string, V> =>
  Object.fromEntries(pairs) satisfies Record<string, V>;

// ✅ GOOD — domain fn calls fromEntries itself
export const formResponseValues = (doc): Record<string, Response> =>
  Object.fromEntries(
    doc.responses.map((r): readonly [string, Response] => [
      r.formItemId,
      r.response,
    ]),
  );
```

Keep a helper only when it owns a rule that can change (e.g.
`keyedResponses` prefers draft over prior). Tuple annotations and
`satisfies` belong at the real call, not in a wrapper invented to hide
them.

**Incident:** `fillViewerExtra` / `reviewViewerExtra` took a raw field bag
and returned `branded({ …same fields… })`. Nesting `setValue`+`value` into
`response` is not a mapping between two known types — it is an object
literal the call site already had. The wrapper added no concision and hid
the real constructor (`branded`).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — typed alias of branded; args are the output fields
export const fillViewerExtra = (args: {
  getChild; error; setValue; value; impRef; …
}): FillItemExtra =>
  branded({
    getChild: args.getChild,
    error: args.error,
    response: { setValue: args.setValue, value: args.value },
    impRef: args.impRef,
    …
  });

// ✅ GOOD — branded at the call that already owns every field
extra: branded({
  getChild,
  error,
  response: { setValue, value },
  impRef,
  …
})
```

If `branded({…})` needs a type argument, pass it **at that call**. Do not
invent a function to hide `branded` or to restitch a bag the caller built.

Related: `no-unsafe-casts.mdc` (do not wrap just to avoid `as`).
